  </div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th>Spread (USD/L)</th><th>Highest Region</th><th>Lowest Region</th></tr>
    {''.join(f'<tr><td>{d["year"]}</td><td>${d["spread"]}</td><td>{yearly_region_pivot.loc[d["year"]].idxmax()}</td><td>{yearly_region_pivot.loc[d["year"]].idxmin()}</td></tr>' for d in spread_data)}
  </table>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: SUBSIDY & TAX ANALYSIS -->
<div class="section" id="subsidy-tax">
  <h2><span class="section-num">04</span>Impact of Subsidies and Taxation on Retail Prices</h2>

  <p>This section evaluates the effectiveness of government interventions — subsidies and taxation — in moderating the retail price consumers pay for fuel, and whether high-subsidy countries demonstrate reduced pass-through from crude oil price fluctuations.</p>

  <h3>High-Subsidy vs. Low-Subsidy Countries</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg LPG (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax %</th></tr>
    {''.join(f'<tr><td><span class="badge {"badge-high" if r["subsidy_level"]=="High" else "badge-low"}">{r["subsidy_level"]}</span></td><td>${r["petrol_mean"]}</td><td>${r["diesel_mean"]}</td><td>${r["lpg_mean"]}</td><td>{r["tax_mean"]}%</td></tr>' for _, r in subsidy_avg.iterrows())}
  </table>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Subsidy Price Dampening Effect: {subsidy_dampening}%</strong><br>
    High-subsidy countries average ${high_sub_petrol}/L for petrol compared to ${low_sub_petrol}/L in low-subsidy countries — a {subsidy_dampening}% reduction attributable to subsidy regimes.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartSubsidy" height="90"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Average retail petrol and diesel prices (USD/L) by subsidy level</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Subsidy & Income Level Interaction</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Income Level</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax %</th></tr>
    {''.join(f'<tr><td><span class="badge {"badge-high" if r["subsidy_level"]=="High" else "badge-low"}">{r["subsidy_level"]}</span></td><td>{r["income_level"]}</td><td>${r["petrol_mean"]}</td><td>${r["diesel_mean"]}</td><td>{r["tax_mean"]}%</td></tr>' for _, r in subsidy_income.sort_values(['subsidy_level', 'income_level']).iterrows())}
  </table>

  <h3>Crude-to-Retail Pass-Through by Subsidy Level</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Brent-to-Petrol Correlation</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-high">High</span></td><td><strong>{pass_through['High']}</strong></td><td>{'Strong' if abs(pass_through['High']) > 0.7 else 'Moderate'} pass-through — subsidies {"partially buffer" if abs(pass_through['High']) < abs(pass_through['Low']) else "do not meaningfully buffer"} crude swings</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-low">Low</span></td><td><strong>{pass_through['Low']}</strong></td><td>{'Strong' if abs(pass_through['Low']) > 0.7 else 'Moderate'} pass-through — retail prices track Brent closely</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Pass-Through Analysis:</strong> High-subsidy countries show a Brent-to-petrol correlation of {pass_through['High']}, compared to {pass_through['Low']} for low-subsidy countries. {'This confirms that subsidies reduce but do not eliminate the transmission of crude price movements to consumers.' if abs(pass_through['High']) < abs(pass_through['Low']) else 'Both groups show similar correlation levels, suggesting that subsidies primarily lower the price level rather than decoupling retail from crude dynamics.'}
  </div>

  <h3>Yearly Price Trajectory by Subsidy Level</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartSubsidyYearly" height="95"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Average retail petrol price (USD/L) by subsidy level, 2020–2026 — the gap between high and low subsidy tracks the dampening effect</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Taxation & Retail Prices</h3>
  <p>The overall correlation between tax percentage and retail petrol price is <strong>{tax_price_corr}</strong>. {'This positive correlation indicates that higher-tax countries tend to have higher retail prices, though the relationship is not determinative — subsidy regimes and other cost factors also play major roles.' if tax_price_corr > 0 else 'This negative correlation suggests that higher-tax countries tend to have lower retail prices, possibly because high-tax countries also tend to have subsidy mechanisms or lower baseline costs.'}</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Avg Tax %</th><th>Median Tax %</th><th>Tax Std Dev</th></tr>
    {''.join(f'<tr><td>{r["income_level"]}</td><td>{r["tax_mean"]}%</td><td>{r["tax_median"]}%</td><td>{r["tax_std"]}</td></tr>' for _, r in tax_by_income.iterrows())}
  </table>
</div>

