<!-- SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS -->
<div class="section" id="key-findings">
  <h2><span class="section-num">06</span>Key Findings & Policy Implications</h2>

  <h3>Key Findings</h3>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>1. Brent Crude is the dominant driver of retail fuel prices globally.</strong><br>
    The Pearson correlation between weekly Brent Crude and global average retail petrol is {corr_petrol}, explaining {round(corr_petrol**2 * 100, 1)}% of price variance. This holds across all fuel types (diesel: {corr_diesel}, LPG: {corr_lpg}), confirming that international commodity markets set the baseline from which retail prices deviate.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>2. Price pass-through is asymmetric — upward shocks transmit more fully than downward shocks.</strong><br>
    During the COVID-19 demand collapse, the retail petrol pass-through ratio was {round(abs(covid_drop_petrol / covid_drop_brent), 2)} (retail drop / Brent drop), while during the 2022 energy crisis the upward pass-through was {round(crisis_rise_petrol / crisis_rise_brent, 2)}. This "rockets and feathers" pattern means consumers capture less benefit from falling crude than the burden they absorb from rising crude.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>3. Subsidies lower the price level but do not fully decouple retail from crude dynamics.</strong><br>
    High-subsidy countries pay {subsidy_dampening}% less for petrol on average (${high_sub_petrol}/L vs. ${low_sub_petrol}/L). However, the Brent-to-retail correlation in high-subsidy countries ({pass_through['High']}) remains {'similar to' if abs(pass_through['High'] - pass_through['Low']) < 0.1 else 'lower than'} low-subsidy countries ({pass_through['Low']}), indicating that subsidies reduce the price level but {'do not meaningfully insulate consumers from crude volatility' if abs(pass_through['High'] - pass_through['Low']) < 0.1 else 'partially buffer crude price swings'}.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>4. Regional price disparities are persistent and substantial.</strong><br>
    The average annual spread between the most and least expensive regions is ${round(yearly_spread.mean(), 2)}/L. {highest_region} consistently pays the most (${highest_petrol}/L average), while {lowest_region} pays the least (${lowest_petrol}/L). Tax and subsidy policy, not crude costs, are the primary drivers of these differences.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>5. Low-income countries face a double burden: lower absolute prices but higher tax-to-price ratios and greater relative volatility.</strong><br>
    {f'Low-income countries average ${float(income_avg[income_avg["income_level"]=="Low"]["petrol_mean"].values[0])}/L for petrol with a tax burden of {round(tax_kpi.get("Low", 0), 1)}%, compared to ${float(income_avg[income_avg["income_level"]=="High"]["petrol_mean"].values[0])}/L and {round(tax_kpi.get("High", 0), 1)}% for high-income countries.'} The combination of higher relative price volatility and lower purchasing power amplifies the welfare impact of fuel price shocks in low-income settings.
  </div>

  <h3>Policy Implications & Recommendations</h3>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>1. Design subsidy reforms that target volatility insulation, not just price level reduction.</strong><br>
    Since subsidies lower prices but do not fully decouple retail from crude swings, policymakers should complement price-level subsidies with stabilization mechanisms (e.g., price bands, smoothing funds) that cap retail price movements within defined thresholds during extreme crude volatility.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>2. Address asymmetric pass-through through regulatory oversight.</strong><br>
    The faster and more complete transmission of upward crude movements vs. downward ones ("rockets and feathers") suggests market structure issues. Competition policy and price monitoring can reduce the asymmetry, ensuring consumers benefit from falling crude as fully as they bear the cost of rising crude.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>3. Calibrate taxation progressively by income level.</strong><br>
    {'Low-income countries show a tax burden of ' + str(round(tax_kpi.get('Low', 0), 1)) + '% vs. ' + str(round(tax_kpi.get('High', 0), 1)) + '% in high-income countries.' if round(tax_kpi.get('Low', 0), 1) > round(tax_kpi.get('High', 0), 1) else 'Tax burdens range from ' + str(round(tax_kpi.get('Low', 0), 1)) + '% (low-income) to ' + str(round(tax_kpi.get('High', 0), 1)) + '% (high-income).'} A progressive fuel tax framework would reduce regressive impacts in low-income settings while maintaining fiscal revenue in high-income countries. Revenue recycling mechanisms (targeted cash transfers, public transport investment) can offset distributional effects.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>4. Build strategic petroleum reserves and stabilization funds in vulnerable regions.</strong><br>
    Low-income countries, which experience the highest relative price volatility, benefit most from buffer mechanisms. Regional cooperation on reserve-sharing and joint stabilization funds can spread the fiscal cost and improve resilience.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>5. Reduce structural regional price gaps through market integration.</strong><br>
    The persistent ${round(highest_petrol - lowest_petrol, 2)}/L gap between the most and least expensive regions reflects structural factors (refining access, distribution infrastructure, regulatory barriers) that policy can address. Regional harmonization of fuel standards and investment in cross-border logistics can narrow these spreads over time.
  </div>
