.stat-box .stat-value {{ font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--primary); margin-top: 4px; }}
.finding {{
  background: #f0f5ff;
  border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
  padding: 14px 18px;
  margin: 12px 0;
  border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
  font-size: 0.94rem;
  color: var(--text-secondary);
  line-height: 1.65;
}}
.finding strong {{ color: var(--primary); }}
.recommendation {{
  background: #f0fff4;
  border-left: 4px solid var(--success);
  padding: 14px 18px;
  margin: 12px 0;
  border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
  font-size: 0.94rem;
  color: var(--text-secondary);
  line-height: 1.65;
}}
.recommendation strong {{ color: #276749; }}
.badge {{ display: inline-block; padding: 3px 10px; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 600; }}
.badge-high {{ background: #fed7d7; color: #9b2c2c; }}
.badge-low {{ background: #c6f6d5; color: #276749; }}
.badge-info {{ background: #bee3f8; color: #2a4365; }}
.footer {{ text-align: center; padding: 32px 24px; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-top: 16px; }}
@media (max-width: 768px) {{
  .kpi-bar {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; margin-top: -16px; }}
  .section {{ padding: 20px 18px; }}
  .report-header h1 {{ font-size: 1.5rem; }}
  .stat-grid {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }}
}}
@media (max-width: 480px) {{
  .kpi-bar {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; }}
  .stat-grid {{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; }}
}}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="report-header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Global Fuel Price Trends &amp; Policy Impact Analysis</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis of retail fuel prices across 84 countries, examining the interplay between Brent Crude benchmarks, taxation, subsidies, and regional economic conditions over the 2020–2026 period.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Coverage: {date_min} &ndash; {date_max}</span>
      <span>Countries: {total_countries}</span>
      <span>Data points: {len(df):,}</span>
      <span>Generated: {datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="kpi-bar">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Retail Petrol Price</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">${avg_petrol}/L</div>
    <div class="kpi-note">Global mean across all countries &amp; weeks</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Brent-to-Retail Correlation</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{corr_petrol}</div>
    <div class="kpi-note">Petrol | Diesel: {corr_diesel}</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Subsidy Dampening Effect</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{subsidy_dampening}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-note">High-subsidy vs. low-subsidy price gap</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Regional Price Spread</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">${round(yearly_spread.mean(), 2)}/L</div>
    <div class="kpi-note">Avg. spread between highest &amp; lowest region</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Tax Burden by Income</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{high_tax}% / {round(tax_kpi.get('Middle', 0), 1)}% / {low_tax}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-note">High / Middle / Low income avg. tax %</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="toc">
  <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#global-overview">Global Fuel Price Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#temporal-trends">Temporal Price Trends &amp; Volatility</a></li>
    <li><a href="#regional-comparison">Regional Price Disparities</a></li>
    <li><a href="#subsidy-tax">Impact of Subsidies and Taxation on Retail Prices</a></li>
    <li><a href="#income-level">Fuel Affordability by Income Level</a></li>
    <li><a href="#key-findings">Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</a></li>
  </ol>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<div class="section" id="global-overview">
  <h2><span class="section-num">01</span>Global Fuel Price Overview</h2>
  <p>This section presents summary statistics for the three retail fuel types and the Brent Crude benchmark across the full study period (January 2020 &ndash; April 2026), spanning {total_countries} countries and {total_weeks} weekly observations.</p>

  <h3>Summary Statistics</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Mean (USD/L)</th><th>Median (USD/L)</th><th>Min (USD/L)</th><th>Max (USD/L)</th><th>Std Dev</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Petrol</strong></td><td>${overall_stats['Petrol']['mean']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Petrol']['median']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Petrol']['min']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Petrol']['max']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Petrol']['std']}</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Diesel</strong></td><td>${overall_stats['Diesel']['mean']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Diesel']['median']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Diesel']['min']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Diesel']['max']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Diesel']['std']}</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>LPG</strong></td><td>${overall_stats['Lpg']['mean']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Lpg']['median']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Lpg']['min']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Lpg']['max']}</td><td>${overall_stats['Lpg']['std']}</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Brent Crude (USD/bbl)</strong></td><td>${brent_stats['mean']}</td><td>${brent_stats['median']}</td><td>${brent_stats['min']}</td><td>${brent_stats['max']}</td><td>${brent_stats['std']}</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Brent Crude Benchmark</h3>
  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Peak Price</div>
      <div class="stat-value">${brent_peak_price}</div>
      <div class="stat-label">{brent_peak_date}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Trough Price</div>
      <div class="stat-value">${brent_trough_price}</div>
      <div class="stat-label">{brent_trough_date}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Full-Period Range</div>
      <div class="stat-value">${round(brent_stats['max'] - brent_stats['min'], 2)}</div>
      <div class="stat-label">Max &ndash; Min spread</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Period Average</div>
      <div class="stat-value">${brent_stats['mean']}</div>
      <div class="stat-label">USD per barrel</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Highest &amp; Lowest Petrol Price Countries (Period Average)</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Country</th><th>Region</th><th>Income Level</th><th>Subsidy</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th></tr>
    {top5_rows}
    <tr><td colspan="6" style="text-align:center; color:var(--text-muted); font-style:italic;">&hellip;</td></tr>
    {bottom5_rows}
  </table>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<div class="section" id="temporal-trends">
  <h2><span class="section-num">02</span>Temporal Price Trends &amp; Volatility</h2>
  <p>This section tracks the evolution of retail fuel prices and Brent Crude over the study period, with particular focus on the two defining disruptions: the COVID-19 demand collapse (2020) and the 2022 energy price crisis.</p>

  <h3>Weekly Brent Crude &amp; Global Average Retail Prices</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartTimeline" height="110"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Weekly Brent Crude (USD/bbl, left axis) vs. global average retail petrol &amp; diesel (USD/L, right axis), January 2020 &ndash; April 2026</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Yearly Average Prices</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th>Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Brent (USD/bbl)</th><th>Petrol Std Dev</th><th>Diesel Std Dev</th><th>Brent CV (%)</th></tr>
    {yearly_table_rows}
  </table>

  <h3>Volatility: Coefficient of Variation by Year</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartVolatility" height="90"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Coefficient of variation (CV%) for petrol retail prices and Brent Crude by year &mdash; higher values indicate greater relative volatility</div>
  </div>

  <h3>COVID-19 Demand Shock (Q1 &rarr; Q2 2020)</h3>
  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Brent Crude Drop</div>
      <div class="stat-value" style="color:var(--danger)">{covid_drop_brent}%</div>
      <div class="stat-label">Q1 &rarr; Q2 2020</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Retail Petrol Drop</div>
      <div class="stat-value" style="color:var(--danger)">{covid_drop_petrol}%</div>
      <div class="stat-label">Q1 &rarr; Q2 2020</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Pass-Through Ratio</div>
      <div class="stat-value">{round(abs(covid_drop_petrol / covid_drop_brent), 2)}</div>
      <div class="stat-label">Retail drop / Brent drop</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>The Brent Crude benchmark fell {abs(covid_drop_brent)}% from Q1 to Q2 2020, while retail petrol prices declined {abs(covid_drop_petrol)}% &mdash; a pass-through ratio of {round(abs(covid_drop_petrol / covid_drop_brent), 2)}, indicating significant but incomplete transmission of the crude price shock to consumers. Subsidy regimes, fixed taxes, and contracting margins all contributed to the dampened retail response.</p>

  <h3>2022 Energy Crisis (H1 2021 &rarr; H1 2022)</h3>
  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Brent Crude Surge</div>
      <div class="stat-value" style="color:var(--danger)">+{crisis_rise_brent}%</div>
      <div class="stat-label">H1 2021 &rarr; H1 2022</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Retail Petrol Surge</div>
      <div class="stat-value" style="color:var(--danger)">+{crisis_rise_petrol}%</div>
      <div class="stat-label">H1 2021 &rarr; H1 2022</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-box">
      <div class="stat-label">Pass-Through Ratio</div>
      <div class="stat-value">{round(crisis_rise_petrol / crisis_rise_brent, 2)}</div>
      <div class="stat-label">Retail rise / Brent rise</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>The 2022 energy crisis saw Brent Crude surge {crisis_rise_brent}% from H1 2021 to H1 2022, while retail petrol prices rose {crisis_rise_petrol}%. The pass-through ratio of {round(crisis_rise_petrol / crisis_rise_brent, 2)} suggests that upward crude movements were transmitted to consumers more completely than the downward shock during COVID, consistent with asymmetric &ldquo;rockets and feathers&rdquo; pricing dynamics.</p>

  <h3>Brent-to-Retail Price Correlation</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Pearson Correlation (r)</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Petrol</td><td><strong>{corr_petrol}</strong></td><td>{corr_petrol_interp} positive correlation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Diesel</td><td><strong>{corr_diesel}</strong></td><td>{corr_diesel_interp} positive correlation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>LPG</td><td><strong>{corr_lpg}</strong></td><td>{corr_lpg_interp} positive correlation</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The correlation analysis confirms that Brent Crude is a strong driver of retail fuel prices globally. The petrol correlation of {corr_petrol} indicates that Brent movements explain approximately {round(corr_petrol**2 * 100, 1)}% of the variance in weekly global average retail petrol prices.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<div class="section" id="regional-comparison">
  <h2><span class="section-num">03</span>Regional Price Disparities</h2>
  <p>Regional analysis reveals persistent disparities in retail fuel prices, driven by differences in taxation, subsidy regimes, refining capacity, and distribution costs.</p>

  <h3>Average Fuel Prices by Region (Full Period)</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartRegional" height="100"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Average retail petrol and diesel prices (USD/L) by geographic region, January 2020 &ndash; April 2026</div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Region</th><th>Countries</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg LPG (USD/L)</th></tr>
    {region_table_rows}
  </table>

  <p>The <strong>{highest_region}</strong> region registers the highest average petrol price at ${highest_petrol}/L, while <strong>{lowest_region}</strong> has the lowest at ${lowest_petrol}/L &mdash; a gap of ${round(highest_petrol - lowest_petrol, 3)}/L.</p>

  <h3>Regional Price Evolution by Year</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartRegionalYearly" height="110"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Average retail petrol price (USD/L) by region, 2020&ndash;2026</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Regional Price Spread Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartSpread" height="85"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Difference between highest and lowest regional average petrol price by year</div>
  </div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th>Spread (USD/L)</th><th>Highest Region</th><th>Lowest Region</th></tr>
    {spread_table_rows}
  </table>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<div class="section" id="subsidy-tax">
  <h2><span class="section-num">04</span>Impact of Subsidies and Taxation on Retail Prices</h2>
  <p>This section evaluates the effectiveness of government interventions &mdash; subsidies and taxation &mdash; in moderating the retail price consumers pay for fuel, and whether high-subsidy countries demonstrate reduced pass-through from crude oil price fluctuations.</p>

  <h3>High-Subsidy vs. Low-Subsidy Countries</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg LPG (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax %</th></tr>
    {subsidy_table_rows}
  </table>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Subsidy Price Dampening Effect: {subsidy_dampening}%</strong><br>
    High-subsidy countries average ${high_sub_petrol}/L for petrol compared to ${low_sub_petrol}/L in low-subsidy countries &mdash; a {subsidy_dampening}% reduction attributable to subsidy regimes.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartSubsidy" height="90"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Average retail petrol and diesel prices (USD/L) by subsidy level</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Subsidy &amp; Income Level Interaction</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Income Level</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax %</th></tr>
    {subsidy_income_rows}
  </table>

  <h3>Crude-to-Retail Pass-Through by Subsidy Level</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th>Brent-to-Petrol Correlation</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-high">High</span></td><td><strong>{pass_through['High']}</strong></td><td>{pt_high_interp} pass-through &mdash; {subsidy_buffer_text}</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-low">Low</span></td><td><strong>{pass_through['Low']}</strong></td><td>{pt_low_interp} pass-through &mdash; retail prices track Brent closely</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Pass-Through Analysis:</strong> {pass_through_finding}
  </div>

  <h3>Yearly Price Trajectory by Subsidy Level</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartSubsidyYearly" height="95"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Average retail petrol price (USD/L) by subsidy level, 2020&ndash;2026</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Taxation &amp; Retail Prices</h3>
  <p>The overall correlation between tax percentage and retail petrol price is <strong>{tax_price_corr}</strong>. {tax_corr_text}</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Avg Tax %</th><th>Median Tax %</th><th>Tax Std Dev</th></tr>
    {tax_income_rows}
  </table>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<div class="section" id="income-level">
  <h2><span class="section-num">05</span>Fuel Affordability by Income Level</h2>
  <p>Segmenting countries by income classification reveals stark differences in fuel pricing, tax burden, and price stability &mdash; with significant implications for energy equity.</p>

  <h3>Average Prices &amp; Tax Burden by Income Level</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartIncome" height="95"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Average retail petrol, diesel prices (USD/L) and tax percentage by income level</div>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Income Level</th><th>Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th>Avg LPG (USD/L)</th><th>Avg Tax %</th><th>Price Volatility (CV%)</th></tr>
    {income_avg_rows}
  </table>

  <h3>Price Evolution by Income Level</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="chartIncomeYearly" height="100"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-caption">Average retail petrol price (USD/L) by income level, 2020&ndash;2026</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Price Stability by Income Level (Annual CV%)</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th>High Income CV%</th><th>Middle Income CV%</th><th>Low Income CV%</th></tr>
    {income_stability_rows}
  </table>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<div class="section" id="key-findings">
  <h2><span class="section-num">06</span>Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>

  <h3>Key Findings</h3>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>1. Brent Crude is the dominant driver of retail fuel prices globally.</strong><br>
    The Pearson correlation between weekly Brent Crude and global average retail petrol is {corr_petrol}, explaining {round(corr_petrol**2 * 100, 1)}% of price variance. This holds across all fuel types (diesel: {corr_diesel}, LPG: {corr_lpg}), confirming that international commodity markets set the baseline from which retail prices deviate.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>2. Price pass-through is asymmetric &mdash; upward shocks transmit more fully than downward shocks.</strong><br>
    During the COVID-19 demand collapse, the retail petrol pass-through ratio was {round(abs(covid_drop_petrol / covid_drop_brent), 2)} (retail drop / Brent drop), while during the 2022 energy crisis the upward pass-through was {round(crisis_rise_petrol / crisis_rise_brent, 2)}. This &ldquo;rockets and feathers&rdquo; pattern means consumers capture less benefit from falling crude than the burden they absorb from rising crude.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>3. Subsidies lower the price level but do not fully decouple retail from crude dynamics.</strong><br>
    High-subsidy countries pay {subsidy_dampening}% less for petrol on average (${high_sub_petrol}/L vs. ${low_sub_petrol}/L). However, the Brent-to-retail correlation in high-subsidy countries ({pass_through['High']}) remains {'similar to' if pt_diff < 0.05 else 'lower than'} low-subsidy countries ({pass_through['Low']}), indicating that subsidies reduce the price level but {subsidy_buffer_text}.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>4. Regional price disparities are persistent and substantial.</strong><br>
    The average annual spread between the most and least expensive regions is ${round(yearly_spread.mean(), 2)}/L. {highest_region} consistently pays the most (${highest_petrol}/L average), while {lowest_region} pays the least (${lowest_petrol}/L). Tax and subsidy policy, not crude costs, are the primary drivers of these differences.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>5. Low-income countries face a double burden: lower absolute prices but higher tax-to-price ratios and greater relative volatility.</strong><br>
    Low-income countries average ${low_income_petrol}/L for petrol with a tax burden of {low_tax}%, compared to ${high_income_petrol}/L and {high_tax}% for high-income countries. The combination of higher relative price volatility and lower purchasing power amplifies the welfare impact of fuel price shocks in low-income settings.
  </div>

  <h3>Policy Implications &amp; Recommendations</h3>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>1. Design subsidy reforms that target volatility insulation, not just price level reduction.</strong><br>
    Since subsidies lower prices but do not fully decouple retail from crude swings, policymakers should complement price-level subsidies with stabilization mechanisms (e.g., price bands, smoothing funds) that cap retail price movements within defined thresholds during extreme crude volatility.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>2. Address asymmetric pass-through through regulatory oversight.</strong><br>
    The faster and more complete transmission of upward crude movements vs. downward ones (&ldquo;rockets and feathers&rdquo;) suggests market structure issues. Competition policy and price monitoring can reduce the asymmetry, ensuring consumers benefit from falling crude as fully as they bear the cost of rising crude.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>3. Calibrate taxation progressively by income level.</strong><br>
    Tax burdens range from {low_tax}% (low-income) to {high_tax}% (high-income). A progressive fuel tax framework would reduce regressive impacts in low-income settings while maintaining fiscal revenue in high-income countries. Revenue recycling mechanisms (targeted cash transfers, public transport investment) can offset distributional effects.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>4. Build strategic petroleum reserves and stabilization funds in vulnerable regions.</strong><br>
    Low-income countries, which experience the highest relative price volatility, benefit most from buffer mechanisms. Regional cooperation on reserve-sharing and joint stabilization funds can spread the fiscal cost and improve resilience.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>5. Reduce structural regional price gaps through market integration.</strong><br>
    The persistent ${round(highest_petrol - lowest_petrol, 2)}/L gap between the most and least expensive regions reflects structural factors (refining access, distribution infrastructure, regulatory barriers) that policy can address. Regional harmonization of fuel standards and investment in cross-border logistics can narrow these spreads over time.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="footer">
